WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.405
- I have a concern about critical theory.

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:04.860
This whole, everything
that's happening right now,

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.730
I believe it was a Trojan horse.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:11.730
I believe that James Lindsay,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.110
and how he described it as a Trojan horse,

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:15.169
he's exactly right on.

00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:17.660
It's coming into our churches.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.609
Dude, the division that it's
causing right now is insane,

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:21.900
because like you said,
it's another gospel.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.510
It's another religion.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.680
It's like, because once again,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.310
they have their own epistemology.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.490
So the way that they discover truth

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.310
is from a positional standpoint.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.539
So how do I respond to that?

00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:37.122
(serene music)

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.510
- I'm Dave Rubin and this
is the "Rubin Report."

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:46.870
Quick reminder, everybody,

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.790
to subscribe to our YouTube channel,

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:51.570
and click that pesky notification bell.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.730
And joining me today is an
author, and a teaching pastor,

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.490
Nathan Finochio, welcome
to the "Rubin Report."

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.799
- Hey, it's good to be here, Dave.

00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:02.940
- I am glad to have you, sir,
because as you probably know,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.313
I do some talking to
people of faith, people,

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.360
that are skeptics, people
that are somewhere in between.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.930
And I always find these to be sort of

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.450
the most interesting
conversations that I have.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.980
So before we do anything
about what you're doing now,

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.803
tell me a little bit about
your journey to faith.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.170
- Yeah, so I'm the son of a preacher man.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.083
My dad has been in the ministry
as long as I could remember.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.370
And I've sort of just fallen
into it, to be quite honest.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.230
I tried to, kind of, not
necessarily run the other way,

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.870
but do something else, and
sorta just caught up to me,

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:44.160
it's like, so it just kind
of, this is what I do now.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.070
'Cause it's just kind of what I know.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.593
But I do,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.280
I've always had a kind
of strong faith in God.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.870
I just never saw myself
as the ministerial type,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.490
because you know, like people of faith,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.612
I guess spiritual leaders,
they kind of have a type.

00:02:01.612 --> 00:02:05.360
in my opinion, they're kind of square,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.250
and they stay home on Friday nights

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.090
and play Scrabble with grandma.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.030
And it was just not what I
wanted to do with my life.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.330
You know?

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:14.610
- Hey, don't knock Scrabble, man.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.160
Everybody loves Scrabble.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.160
- Look, I take that back, okay?

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.683
Scrabble is great, and
grandma's wonderful.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.430
- Well, to your point though, you kinda,

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.170
it's like, well, wait a
minute, look at this guy.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:27.830
He doesn't really look like a pastor.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:28.710
And then on the other hand,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.520
you look like sort of a modern Jesus.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.871
So you're sort of doing a
little everything over there.

00:02:33.871 --> 00:02:36.523
- It's, you know, it's a work in progress.

00:02:37.372 --> 00:02:40.980
- (laughing) Did you, on the way,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.020
you said you did a little something

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.060
before going into this,

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.690
even though you came
from a family of faith.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.860
Was the faith part always there,

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.080
or did you mean that you
deviated from that too?

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.010
Or did you just mean that

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:53.530
sort of career wise or something else?

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.102
- I just meant that sort
of career wise, yeah.

00:02:55.102 --> 00:02:59.180
I was doing music for about eight years.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.530
And so yeah, kinda doing that,

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.700
and things just didn't work out

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.280
the way I kind of wanted them to work out.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.153
I sort of went back to,

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.500
went back to the family farm, so to speak.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.990
And so I'm just kind
of a doing ministry now

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:17.933
for the last gosh, I
dunno, last six years.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:22.000
- So if somebody said
to you sort of lay out

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.670
what your belief system
is, what would that be?

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.680
Kinda broadly speaking.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.163
- Yeah, so I'm a, I'm a
confessional Christian.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.280
That's what I'd say.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.280
I'm in the kind of the
brand of Christianity,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.850
that I'm, or the stream that I flow in,

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.940
it's more of a Pentecostal
Christianity, you know?

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:44.790
So we're the ones that, I mean,

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.480
I've never handled a snake before,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.710
but that's kind of what
Pentecostals are known for.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:54.500
Tambourine destroying Pentecostals,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.490
we're the ones that when
you go to our churches,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.420
on the weekend, it's
kinda like a rock concert.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.430
So we've got lasers and you know,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.020
we kind of attract a younger crowd.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.630
But our idea basically is that we have

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:12.630
a historic Orthodox
confessional Christian doctrine.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:16.170
So we believe that the stuff

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.240
that the church has always believed,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.480
and we confess and we profess that.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.480
But we wrap it up in sort of
a modern, in a modern vibe.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.860
- Yeah, do you get a
lot of pushback on that?

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.380
Sort of, I've seen this in
a few different versions,

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.380
where there's the sort of belief

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:37.920
that you just have to have the belief,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.850
and the orthodoxy and the
conservative look at religion,

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.630
and then you bring in
music, you bring in lasers,

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.950
you bring in bands, and
all sorts of other stuff.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.450
And people, a certain set of people

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.760
will think that that will kind of erode

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.023
the original message.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.610
- Yeah, yeah, I think

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.820
that that's utter and
total ridiculousness.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.100
Basically, I mean look at Vatican Three,

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.620
and kind of what the
Catholic church has done,

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.760
like is having services in English

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.410
somehow diminish the Catholic churches,

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:12.680
you know, their their message?

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.513
No, it doesn't.

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:15.790
So that's essentially what we're doing,

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:17.910
whereas they're just they're...

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.710
For them, progress is translating the mass

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:22.550
from Latin to English.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.380
For us, we're, we're doing exactly that.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.740
We're translating the message, but like,

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:30.360
the currency, I think of our generation

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.540
is music and the arts.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.923
And so that's kind of how we
communicated that language.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:38.850
- Right, so sort of speaking
to music and the arts,

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.620
what kind of put you on my radar

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.090
is that Justin Bieber is
a member of the church.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.210
I don't want you to get into any specifics

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.480
about anyone that goes to the church,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.480
but what do you sense is
happening with people of his age,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.560
related to belief?

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.650
There does seem to be something going on,

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:00.320
you know, Kanye is running
these services now,

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.960
and as you just said,

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.270
it's sort of related to music and culture,

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:05.103
and something else.

00:06:05.103 --> 00:06:06.883
It's not what we think
of when we just think of

00:06:06.883 --> 00:06:09.570
like the traditional
religious perspective.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:13.230
- Yeah, 100%, I think that people,

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.200
I know you mentioned Justin and Kanye,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.720
as far as I know, they
had church upbringings.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.103
So there's a lot of people
come up in the church.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:24.593
I know Justin for a fact, did.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:28.450
I went to, I spoke at his
home church in Canada.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:29.320
I'm actually Canadian,

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.093
I've I grew up 45 minutes
from where he grew up.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:38.640
So I know like, I mean,
Elvis, for crying out loud,

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:39.590
came up in the church.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.717
It's not uncommon in the American story

00:06:41.717 --> 00:06:44.460
that people in the arts come up in church,

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:46.200
and then they kind of
leave the small town,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.570
and they go do big musical things.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:51.290
So, but in terms of a return,

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:54.000
I mean, we saw it even in Elvis's career.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.910
The only album that he
ever won a Grammy for

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.760
was for his gospel record.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:03.760
So there's something, I
think, intrinsic to Americana,

00:07:04.342 --> 00:07:07.400
and the church and music.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.540
So it's a pretty common theme.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.240
Why millennials and gen Z-ers

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.530
are reconnecting with the church,

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.530
or connecting for the first time to faith,

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:21.793
I think large in part due to the failure,

00:07:21.793 --> 00:07:26.250
perhaps of secularism.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:30.180
It doesn't work and
there's questions, I think,

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.290
that it's attempted to answer,
that it hasn't answered.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.100
And that's why you'll even
see guys like Sam Harris,

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.860
who have become spiritual,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:40.610
not in any sort of Orthodox sense,

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.460
but an awareness that maybe
there is something out there,

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.363
that we connect to, in
soul and spirit, et cetera.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.481
So I think we have
something to offer people

00:07:50.481 --> 00:07:55.481
that is reasonable, and
that is also experiential.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:01.500
- So one of the things that
I've been talking about

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:02.380
on the show a lot lately,

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:04.310
is that I think a lot of
the problems we're having

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:07.460
is that we seem to be reaching
the end of secularism,

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.350
which I think is sort of
what you're alluding to,

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:13.290
that when you get to the
end of pure secularism,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.740
with nothing else outside of itself,

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:17.640
that everything's on the table again.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:19.790
And we don't know if men are
men, and women are women,

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:23.030
and we're debating, destroying our past

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.370
in the name of some utopian future,

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:26.203
and the rest of it.

00:08:26.203 --> 00:08:29.580
Do you see that as just like
the natural kind of outgrowth

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.943
of what a purely secular society is?

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.830
- Yeah, I think, gosh,

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.980
without getting into
a huge history lesson,

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:42.723
what I would call,

00:08:42.723 --> 00:08:45.980
I think there's two types of people

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.000
in our churches right now.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.783
Okay, there's we might call leftists,

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:52.900
and a secular might be,

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:55.113
a secularist might be a better term.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:59.640
But, and then we have,
for lack of a better term,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.970
a conservative, which
I can't stand the term,

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:05.010
but just using generalizations.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.290
And what I think has happened as a society

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:10.500
is that we have an epistemological crisis.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.550
That's kind of what
happened, what has happened,

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:14.250
because secularism is
the idea, essentially,

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.790
that material is the only
thing that you can touch.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.850
And we should all live together
in our material society.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:25.430
And let's forget about
things that are non-material,

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.810
like spirituality, because those
things will just divide us.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.610
And it kind of works out at first,

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:35.010
but then it begins to
hammer down everybody

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.970
into just material, and
people aren't just material.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:39.530
And the world isn't just material.

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.960
There's a lot more to it than that.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.450
And so we have this epistemology

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.480
that's kind of developed from secularism,

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:48.710
and that epistemology is essentially...

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.550
When we got rid of a
traditional authority,

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:57.030
which, traditional authorities like kings,

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.150
and the priests and church,

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:04.150
and not, they weren't
like doing bad things,

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:06.929
and that there was reason for them,

00:10:06.929 --> 00:10:09.633
for us to be suspicious of it,
and so we did away with it.

00:10:09.633 --> 00:10:12.020
It's sort of like now that we
have no traditional authority,

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.580
the self has become the
locus of everything.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:17.890
Like we're the locus of interpretation.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:20.560
And so there's a great deal of pressure

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:23.060
on people to know everything.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:24.293
That's why, like, in our culture,

00:10:24.293 --> 00:10:28.280
you have to self authenticate,
you have to self help.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:29.650
You need to build up the self,

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.240
and you need to be the
best version of yourself,

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:32.960
because nobody out here is gonna help you,

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.940
and you can't trust anybody.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:37.850
And so what happens is that experience

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.230
now becomes like, it
becomes the highest metric,

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:45.810
in a world where there's no
authorities that can be trusted.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.590
And so in the church,

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:48.870
we have people that are coming in,

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:51.640
and it's all about experience.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:55.170
And so that's, I think where,
to borrow from James Lindsay,

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:59.131
that's where a positional
epistemology becomes so massive.

00:10:59.131 --> 00:11:01.140
We're dealing with it
with churches right now.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.200
So it's like, people
love the idea of Jesus,

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:07.050
but the scriptures, to which,

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.556
so Christians, we appeal
to an external authority.

00:11:09.556 --> 00:11:13.406
We believe things about reality,

00:11:13.406 --> 00:11:17.880
that are just kind of,
they're incompatible

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:19.850
with most kids that are coming in.

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:21.517
Like kids that are coming in are going,

00:11:21.517 --> 00:11:23.440
"Reality is my experience."

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:26.760
But Christianity is going,
"Reality is what God says

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:28.390
in the scriptures."

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:29.957
And so that's, it's kind of...

00:11:29.957 --> 00:11:31.950
And it's very similar to,

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:33.180
that's why I think the Christianity

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:34.870
will save Western civilization,

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.520
because we might be the last ones

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:39.543
who believe in science,
which would be hilarious.

00:11:40.494 --> 00:11:43.220
(laughing) But just the idea that,

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:48.220
we believe that that
truth exists externally.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:51.850
Like imagine that religious fanatics

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:55.370
believe that truth
externally, Dave, right.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.910
We believe in what we might call

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:00.430
phenomenological transcendence.

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:02.440
The idea that like a tree isn't,

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:04.530
you can't just call a tree
whatever you wanna call a tree.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:05.910
You know, a tree is real.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:06.900
And if you wanna go ahead

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:09.050
and drive your car into that tree,

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:11.730
you will experience
phenomenological transcendence.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:13.230
You will agree with that.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:15.090
But rather, why don't you live your life

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:17.980
like there are things
that exist outside of you,

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:21.690
and for us, the way that
we appeal to Christians,

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.240
we try to kind of try to
convert them to Christianity,

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.900
because Christianity is
this thing that's like,

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.680
it's reasonable, we believe
that truth is external.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:32.640
It's not all about you,
and your experiences.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:36.940
It's also about some things
that God has said in his Word.

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:38.770
And so morality isn't just kind of

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:40.570
whatever you want it to be,

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:42.008
or however you want to paint it.

00:12:42.008 --> 00:12:46.480
It's God who created you,
he has some opinions,

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.640
he's God, it is what it is.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:50.870
And so you need to, does that make sense?

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:54.330
So it's an epistemological
war that the church is in.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.420
- Yeah, you gave me
about five roads there,

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:57.880
that we could shoot off of.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:00.200
So I'm gonna try to come
around to all of them,

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:01.033
or get them into something.

00:13:01.033 --> 00:13:02.521
- Let's do it all.
- But first.

00:13:02.521 --> 00:13:04.730
- (laughing) I'll see if I can do it.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.480
But first, you mentioned James Lindsay,

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:07.900
who I've had on the show, of course,

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:10.180
who wrote the book with Peter Boghossian,

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:12.560
about how to have important conversations.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:14.900
One of the things that
I'm sort of amazed by,

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:18.040
by the sort of James
Lindsay, Peter Boghossian,

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:21.870
Michael Shermer, the more
I would say left leaning,

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:24.480
largely atheist crew

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.277
is that many of them are suddenly going,

00:13:27.277 --> 00:13:29.910
"All those religious people
we were screaming about,

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:31.240
they're not so bad."

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:34.083
And they're appearing on
stage with religious people.

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:36.780
They're, I don't know if you know,

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:39.334
but just about six or eight months ago,

00:13:39.334 --> 00:13:42.660
I was on stage in front
of 14,000 students,

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:44.220
at Liberty University,

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.010
with Jerry Falwell Jr. right next to me,

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.310
and it was just absolutely incredible.

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:50.020
And sure, we have some differences.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.240
We have some theological differences.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.370
We have some, you know,
spiritual differences,

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.420
and political differences,
but nobody cared.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:58.920
Everybody was thrilled, it was wonderful.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:00.640
But I'm curious what you think about that,

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.880
the fact that some of
the sort of main drivers

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:07.040
of secularism, are suddenly
looking at you guys,

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:09.973
basically and going,
"Huh, you're a lot nicer

00:14:09.973 --> 00:14:12.448
than that other crew over there."

00:14:12.448 --> 00:14:16.630
- Yeah, I don't think it's
just modern intersectionalism

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:20.180
that we're strange bedfellows.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.800
I think that we have a
very similar epistemology,

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.710
and that's why we actually argue.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:26.810
Dave, you can't argue with people

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:29.030
that you don't have the
same epistemology with.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:31.880
We have the same epistemology as atheists.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:35.083
Like we do have demands of literature,

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.240
and we come at things believing

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:42.580
that there are external forces.

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:45.670
Okay, the difference being
that an atheist would believe

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:49.140
in a strictly material universe.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:53.450
But material, is super
important to Christians as well.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.210
And the idea that truth lives externally,

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.530
is it's really important to us as well.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:02.600
So I think that yeah, we find
ourselves on the same aisle

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.510
with respect to our epistemology,

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:08.190
and you know this about, I
mean, Christians aren't perfect.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:11.240
And, you know, we've
slammed in our thousands,

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:13.490
and we've made some massive mistakes.

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:16.820
And one of my favorite quotes
of a Christian theologian,

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:18.367
is St. Augustine, who said that,

00:15:18.367 --> 00:15:21.510
"The church is a whore,
but she's my mother."

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:23.890
We're aware, Dave, of the things

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:27.070
that have happened in
history that are stupid.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:29.840
There's a lot of people
who've used the scripture

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:32.163
to do some really stupid things.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.690
But yet we find ourselves now

00:15:38.418 --> 00:15:41.715
debating with people who we
have the same epistemology.

00:15:41.715 --> 00:15:43.210
I can't have a conversation with you,

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:47.140
if you can't define reality
the way that I define reality.

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:50.713
And so to me, once again, it's
an epistemological factor.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:55.760
- Do you sense young people
that are showing up now,

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:57.880
sure, the lights and
the music is something,

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:01.300
and you're explaining sort
of the end of secularism,

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:03.510
so they feel that there's a hole there.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:06.100
But do you sense just like
in the last couple years,

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:07.480
maybe because of the cultural war,

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:11.480
because of social media,
that the hole that they have

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.370
seems to be way bigger,

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:14.900
that it's maybe not just spiritual,

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.200
but it's also very community based.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.540
You know, we got all of
these tools and apps.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:22.020
and we thought it was gonna
make us more connected.

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.750
And in many ways, social
media made us antisocial,

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.010
and now people are
trying to find community

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:29.080
in oddly more traditional ways,

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.320
whether there's lasers or not.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:32.930
- (laughing) Right.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:35.470
Yeah, absolutely, I
remember there was a book

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:38.695
written in the '90s by a guy named Putnam,

00:16:38.695 --> 00:16:41.660
the title was "Bowling Alone."

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.340
And it sort of was a sociological book,

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:47.750
just looking at the erosion
of community in America,

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:49.760
beginning with the bowling leagues.

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:51.400
It felt like bowling leagues where like,

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:56.358
they replaced church for my
grandfather's generation.

00:16:56.358 --> 00:16:59.280
And now yeah, that's gone.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:02.810
So there's definitely
a sociological factor.

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:04.180
I think that, to be quite honest with you,

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:06.440
the sociological factor may very well be

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:08.930
one of the biggest factors in the church.

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:11.530
We're all, we understand
the value of community.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:15.090
We believe theologically that
we're not made to be alone.

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:18.550
There was one thing in
the garden that was wrong.

00:17:18.550 --> 00:17:21.860
A lot of people think that, you
know, in that Genesis story,

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:24.470
that God creates this garden
and it's like, it's perfect,

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:27.290
it's paradise, well, it's not,
it was optimal for living.

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:30.380
And the one thing that was
wrong was that Adam was alone.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:35.320
And so he creates Eve, and so
we're created for one another.

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:36.980
And we need one another desperately.

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:39.470
So that's definitely
a tenant of our faith.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:43.550
And there's definitely a
sociological it factor to church.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.290
And yeah, there's a giant...

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:47.190
I also believe that...

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:54.000
there's this book written
by a Pulitzer Prize winner.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.440
I think his name is Ernest Becker,

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:58.935
and the title of it was
"The Denial of Death."

00:17:58.935 --> 00:17:59.960
In his book, he's,

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:03.710
looking at secularism,

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:06.520
and kind of what it's done in modernity,

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.713
And he believes that
with the death of God,

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:13.720
society sort of was just
like, okay, well now I,

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:18.190
because I need somebody
to validate my reality,

00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:20.420
to validate my existence,

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:24.360
to see me, because we need
to be seen as human beings.

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:25.760
And that's why we need relationship.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:28.110
And so he believed that the first option

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:30.923
that modern stride was the love option,

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:34.560
in that, and that's why
everybody talks about

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:35.930
having the one, finding the one,

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:40.930
like 300 years ago, like
love wasn't the reason

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:42.851
why you got married.

00:18:42.851 --> 00:18:46.210
But now we put all this emphasis,

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:49.250
and that's why we have
apocalyptic hookup culture,

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:53.214
you know, because we're
all trying to find God,

00:18:53.214 --> 00:18:58.214
and for somebody to validate your living.

00:18:58.339 --> 00:19:00.887
So to me, that's what the church does.

00:19:00.887 --> 00:19:02.960
- Are you saying God's not on Tinder?

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:04.080
Is that what you're telling me?

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:06.097
- Oh, he's definitely
on Tinder (laughing).

00:19:09.102 --> 00:19:12.473
He's definitely searching for people.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:17.193
He's going after, he's left
the 99 and going after the one.

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:23.170
So I think that what you
said is absolutely right,

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:26.610
in a roundabout way, I think
that we're looking for love,

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.490
we're looking for romance.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:31.810
And I think we've, in a strange way,

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:33.300
we do find it in community.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:36.253
We find that validation that we need.

00:19:36.253 --> 00:19:40.220
But in a more spiritual sense, Dave.

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:43.780
Yeah, we find it in God, in that he,

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:45.380
and that's what I believe,
and that's what we teach.

00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:47.340
We teach that God's the one who sees you.

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:50.190
He's the one who goes after you.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:52.320
You're not the one who
has been going after him,

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:53.920
but he's been the one
that's been pursuing you,

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:56.180
because he knows you, he
knows how to get to you,

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:57.340
and he loves you.

00:19:57.340 --> 00:20:00.173
So when people sort of,
when that settles them,

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:03.010
there's a lot of things
in their life that change,

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:07.280
and being a part of a community
is definitely one of them.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.820
- Yeah, so before you
mentioned the sort of lefty

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:14.850
verse conservative demographics

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:16.504
that you're dealing with in the church,

00:20:16.504 --> 00:20:19.870
so as we've watched this
culture war just like explode,

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:23.000
all over the place, I know
that all sorts of churches,

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:26.210
temples, I have virtually
every organization,

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:28.240
secular or not, is dealing with

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:30.130
some sort of like internal split

00:20:30.130 --> 00:20:31.490
over how to deal

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:33.390
with everything that's
happening these days,

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:36.160
literally from coronavirus
to Black Lives Matter,

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:38.230
and everything in between.

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:40.683
What version of that are
you seeing these days?

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:47.830
- Yeah, there's definitely a
political part of everything.

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:50.330
I mean, dude, this is insane.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:52.990
Like it's insane, I keep
on telling my friends this.

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.555
I'm sure that all your
friends are just going,

00:20:54.555 --> 00:20:56.767
"What is happening in 2020?"

00:20:58.593 --> 00:21:02.400
There's a conversation in the
church right now, politically,

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:07.400
about the threshold of religious liberty,

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:14.200
versus government needing to keep tabs,

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:18.700
and a lid on coronavirus.

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:21.723
And so we're kind of, I guess,
exploring that threshold.

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:24.880
You got people who...

00:21:27.589 --> 00:21:29.890
That absolutely falls along
ideological lines, right?

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.490
So it seems like there's
two types of Americans.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:34.560
There's ones that want to,

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.830
they have a huge trust in the government.

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.810
My wife is one of 'em.

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.010
My wife is an Australian, I'm a Canadian,

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:44.040
but my wife is like, she
trusts the government.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:45.280
She's just like, "They are, you know what,

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:48.110
they love us, and I want to
be," she told me last night,

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:49.840
she's like, "I wanna be nannied."

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.475
And I'm like, "My God, woman."

00:21:52.475 --> 00:21:56.380
(laughing) I was just like,
"Have you read history?"

00:21:56.380 --> 00:21:57.710
- Good luck with all of that.

00:21:57.710 --> 00:22:01.480
- Totally, so you got my wife, Jasmine,

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:02.530
and then you've got me.

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:06.833
And I'm like the most
distrusting person of government,

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:13.340
because yeah, who are these angels?

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:14.630
You know, like the old argument,

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:15.770
who are these angels that are gonna make

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:16.800
all these right decisions for us?

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:18.780
So there's that's a huge
conversation of the church,

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:22.690
and that alone, Dave,
was tearing us apart.

00:22:22.690 --> 00:22:24.210
Right, like so you got like pastors,

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:25.410
like calling other pastors,

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:26.550
or people calling up people,

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:29.387
like in the faith
community, just being like,

00:22:29.387 --> 00:22:30.780
"You need to trust the government,

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:32.400
authority's from God."

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:37.400
And then now you have, of
course, George Floyd's death,

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:40.143
which was horrific, and tragic.

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:44.843
And you have Black Lives Matter,

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:50.940
the organization that's
kind of occupied some space

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:54.170
in wake of the George Floyd killing,

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:58.663
and then Ahmaud Arbery, because it looks,

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:03.480
from social media,

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:07.395
that young black men are
being hunted down and killed.

00:23:07.395 --> 00:23:12.110
So yeah, you have this
major space and it's,

00:23:12.110 --> 00:23:14.180
this is another thing that's
kind of dividing us right now.

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:16.360
It's just like the narrative to believe

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:19.280
and what we ought to do about it.

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:23.543
And regardless of if you
believe that narrative or not,

00:23:23.543 --> 00:23:25.790
Christians are called to
mourn with those who mourn,

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:27.618
and weep with those who weep.

00:23:27.618 --> 00:23:29.990
So finding that threshold of going, okay,

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:33.283
I might not agree with
the political narrative,

00:23:34.250 --> 00:23:39.250
but I'm called to stand alongside
people who are in my life,

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:44.180
that regardless of whether or
not I believe that they are,

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:46.310
their emotions are rooted
in any kind of reality,

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:49.210
and I'm arguing from a,
if you're a conservative,

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:52.410
like I have a responsibility
towards them to love them.

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:56.080
You know, like my wife is
terrified of airplanes.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:59.206
It's not rooted in
anything logical at all,

00:23:59.206 --> 00:24:02.340
but I'm the one that's
stuck holding the bag,

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.190
and comforting her and getting her Xanax,

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:05.280
when we're on a flight.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:07.430
And, you know, so there's
an element of that,

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:09.560
if you're a conservative,

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:12.380
that you have a responsibility
towards the people

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:14.013
that you're in community with,

00:24:14.013 --> 00:24:15.660
that you're living life with,

00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:20.660
and quoting stats at my wife

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:23.735
doesn't help her flight
anxiety (laughing).

00:24:23.735 --> 00:24:26.350
- Right.
- So finding that threshold

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:28.890
- How worried are you?
- is the mess right now.

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:30.660
- Yeah, how worried are you though,

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:34.400
that letting in this sort of different,

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:37.180
what I would call a
religious belief system,

00:24:37.180 --> 00:24:40.730
social justice, that once
it enters the institution,

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:43.340
enters the church, that in many ways

00:24:43.340 --> 00:24:46.210
it will just, it will rot from the inside,

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:48.080
all of the good things that
you've been talking about.

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:49.780
I mean, this is what
we've sort of seen happen

00:24:49.780 --> 00:24:51.057
to every secular institution.

00:24:51.057 --> 00:24:52.900
It's happening to "The New York Times."

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:54.400
It's happening to "The Washington Post,"

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:56.280
it's happening to Harvard and Princeton,

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:59.000
and all of our liberal institutions.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:01.480
So I'm sort of fascinated where,

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.100
with this idea that it could be entering,

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:06.890
sort of conservative or
religious institutions,

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:08.500
and that they may not even have

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:12.270
the correct defense
mechanisms to purge it,

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:13.265
or keep it at bay.

00:25:13.265 --> 00:25:15.800
- That's exactly what's happening.

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:19.980
So it's not necessarily, so for me,

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.130
I've tried to dance this line where like,

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:25.980
the idea of Black Lives Matter is like,

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:28.320
it's an idea, it's a saying.

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:31.303
I've sort of divorced it
from the organization.

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:33.990
They don't like that,

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:35.350
but I've divorced it from the organization

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:37.260
because, you know, yeah,
Black lives do matter.

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:39.230
And so if there are instances

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:43.210
of where there's police brutality,

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:46.130
that doesn't seem to fit the rest,

00:25:46.130 --> 00:25:48.260
I mean, you know, as we examine the data,

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:49.540
that's one of the big ones.

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:52.780
We know that the Harvard Rolands,

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:58.373
his paper, and we know like
the study that was done,

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:02.190
Heather McDonald obviously
lays this all out.

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:05.410
But one of the things that I
have a giant question mark over

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:08.360
is the police brutality part.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:10.870
I don't know if we're nailing that well.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:14.007
And so let's say that you're
like me, where you're going,

00:26:14.007 --> 00:26:17.650
"Okay, the shooting part,
there's no racial bias

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:20.367
That's been detected by
three major studies."

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:24.500
But police brutality, do
have a question on that,

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:28.200
like why are 30% of Blacks being accused

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:29.033
of resisting arrest?

00:26:29.033 --> 00:26:30.600
Are they resisting, is
that what's happening,

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:35.450
or are the police being antagonistic

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:36.283
towards 'em or whatever.

00:26:36.283 --> 00:26:39.120
So let's just say for example,

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:42.020
that there is some antagonism,
it needs to be dialed back.

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:49.060
So I stand with my brother who feels,

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:50.330
he's terrified, okay.

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:51.930
And he's like, "Nate, you know,

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:53.607
like I just feel like Black Lives Matter."

00:26:53.607 --> 00:26:55.780
"Okay, man, Black Lives Matter.

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:57.870
Okay, I'm with ya Black
Lives," like I'll say that.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:26:58.920
Now the organization,

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:03.030
they believe in the
denuclearization of the family,

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:05.140
which is doing nobody a service,

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:07.690
particularly the Black
community in America, right?

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:12.630
So for me, obviously I have

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:15.423
some fatter theological concerns to fry,

00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:19.378
but I'm walking this line
where I'm in community

00:27:19.378 --> 00:27:22.660
with my Black brothers and sisters.

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:26.206
And so for me, like once again,

00:27:26.206 --> 00:27:29.677
I have a concern about critical theory.

00:27:29.677 --> 00:27:32.510
Everything that's happening right now,

00:27:32.510 --> 00:27:34.370
I believe it was a Trojan horse.

00:27:34.370 --> 00:27:39.370
I believe that that James Lindsay,

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:41.760
and how he described it as a Trojan horse,

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:45.300
he's exactly right on, it's
coming into our churches.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:47.670
Dude, the division that it's
causing right now is insane,

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.540
because like you said,
it's another gospel.

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:51.150
It's another religion.

00:27:51.150 --> 00:27:53.320
It's like, because once again,

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:54.960
they have their own epistemology.

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:57.130
So the way that they discover truth

00:27:57.130 --> 00:27:59.960
is from a positional standpoint.

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:01.853
So how do I respond to that?

00:28:02.716 --> 00:28:05.660
It's almost an impossible dance,

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:09.740
but I have to show rational
compassion, not empathy,

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:14.740
because empathy is, as we've seen,

00:28:15.280 --> 00:28:18.470
in the last, like 10,
15 years in psychology.

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:21.320
is dangerous, and your
biases come into empathy.

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:23.570
I have to have what's
called rational compassion.

00:28:23.570 --> 00:28:27.250
So I have to reasonably
move through the data,

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:29.410
move through the things
that are happening,

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:32.220
using my Christian
worldview and framework,

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:34.093
and my epistemology.

00:28:34.979 --> 00:28:38.947
And then as I'm checking
these movements and going,

00:28:38.947 --> 00:28:40.120
"Okay, these are their demands, right?"

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:41.430
'Cause everything has a demand.

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:46.017
We demand that you overhaul,

00:28:46.017 --> 00:28:48.140
James say you can't tear down

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:49.530
the master's house with
the master's tools.

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:53.820
So we have to completely
change how we structure,

00:28:53.820 --> 00:28:57.600
not just the way we structure
our church organization,

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:00.200
but we also demand that like the way

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:04.430
that you go about finding
truth changes as well.

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:07.600
And so at that point, for me,

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:09.030
I'm not gonna fight peripherals.

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:10.420
Like, in any good argument,

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:11.820
you always argue first principles.

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:12.950
You don't argue peripherals.

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:15.200
And so for me, when it comes to

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:17.440
the Black Lives Matter movement,

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:19.800
when it comes to things that
are happening on the streets,

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:20.910
and the protests, et cetera,

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:23.170
I'm not gonna, there are some things

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:24.290
I think that are peripheral.

00:29:24.290 --> 00:29:27.340
And as conservatives, we get
a little bit sidetracked by.

00:29:27.340 --> 00:29:30.060
And so to me, the critical theory thing,

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:31.910
and this is why I think James Lindsay

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:35.330
is just so on the money right now,

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:38.430
is because it's the, you
don't go after the fruit,

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:40.090
you go after the root.

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:42.130
And to me, critical theory is the root

00:29:42.130 --> 00:29:43.369
of this giant problem.

00:29:43.369 --> 00:29:45.780
It's the reason why that
the political narrative

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:46.647
has been spun the way it is.

00:29:46.647 --> 00:29:49.090
And so to me, that's what I'm going after,

00:29:49.090 --> 00:29:51.823
while holding the hand of
people that are hurting.

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:57.020
- Yeah. I mean, it's
almost an impossible place

00:29:57.020 --> 00:30:00.010
to put yourself because you're
trying to hold something,

00:30:00.010 --> 00:30:01.140
you're trying to be open.

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:03.880
You're trying to retain ideas

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:06.560
and also not put people down, it's tough.

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:08.980
What do you make of this idea,

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:11.890
so you hit on that critical theory,

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:13.370
whatever, cultural Marxism,

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:14.560
whatever you wanna call this thing,

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.080
that it's religious in nature.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:19.960
One of the facets that
I'm constantly amazed by

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:22.110
is there's no forgiveness in this thing,

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:25.990
that basically, if you're
white or you're cisgendered,

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:27.860
or you're Christian or whatever,

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:30.100
you're privileged and thus bad,

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:32.440
and that really, you
just have to pay penance

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:34.110
your whole life, you have
to live on your knees,

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:35.110
your whole life.

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:40.040
Where most religions have some
level of forgiveness, right.

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:42.480
You can actually atone for your sins,

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:44.434
and hopefully be a better
person on the other side.

00:30:44.434 --> 00:30:46.470
This thing doesn't have that.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:48.530
This thing has, oh well,

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:51.540
live like a slave forever, in effect.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:56.540
- Right, 100%, so in critical theory,

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:02.653
you're sort of guilty
before proven innocent.

00:31:03.820 --> 00:31:05.163
So everybody's guilty.

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:11.160
And as James Lindsay has
observed in his findings,

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:15.210
all of the critical
theorists believe essentially

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:16.340
that if you will even,

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:17.784
even for example, if I wanna be,

00:31:17.784 --> 00:31:20.983
somebody accused me of
problematic allyship yesterday.

00:31:22.210 --> 00:31:23.540
But that's the whole point,

00:31:23.540 --> 00:31:25.890
the whole point is that I can never be

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:27.350
truly an ally.

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:30.184
My allyship is always
gonna be problematic.

00:31:30.184 --> 00:31:33.009
There's like, so there's no,

00:31:33.009 --> 00:31:35.880
there's no end to it.

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:39.980
It just continues to be what it is.

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:44.130
So, yeah, they don't forgive,

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:45.970
because there's nothing to forgive,

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:49.330
because I'm stuck in the oppressor group.

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:50.570
If I'm stuck in the oppressor group,

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:51.800
and there's no escaping it,

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:54.830
there can be no forgiveness if
there's no repentance, right?

00:31:54.830 --> 00:31:56.430
Like, isn't that how it works?

00:31:56.430 --> 00:31:59.050
So that I'm just perpetually a sinner,

00:31:59.050 --> 00:32:00.120
and I'm just going to continue

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:02.570
to perpetuate the oppressor group.

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:04.130
And there's nothing that I can do.

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:05.885
Yeah, so that's why, of course,

00:32:05.885 --> 00:32:09.350
cancel culture is actually
the logical conclusion

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:11.290
of critical theory, right?

00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:12.280
It's the logical conclusion,

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:15.480
that to be consistent logically,

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:19.170
they have to be consistent
in their cancel culture,

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:21.770
because they have to get
rid of the oppressor class.

00:32:22.700 --> 00:32:23.533
- I hate to tell you,

00:32:23.533 --> 00:32:25.800
I think it's beheadings
is the logical conclusion.

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:26.847
Cancel culture is just,

00:32:26.847 --> 00:32:29.520
cancel culture is just a stop on the way,

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:30.847
but we can sort of leave that.

00:32:30.847 --> 00:32:34.570
Just for you personally,
dealing with this,

00:32:34.570 --> 00:32:36.750
the idea that everything's
become political,

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:39.450
and in many ways, I think people used to

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:41.800
go to have religious experiences,

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:42.850
regardless of the religion,

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:45.140
they would go there to escape politics,

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:48.000
that that religion sort of offered them

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:51.070
that separation from the real world,

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:55.030
to think of something
timeless, something like that.

00:32:55.030 --> 00:32:56.510
To see it all seep in,

00:32:56.510 --> 00:32:58.990
just for you as a leader of a community,

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:00.140
How's that been on you?

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:07.200
- Yeah, so one of the things that I do,

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:11.073
in my role, is I travel
to a number of churches.

00:33:11.073 --> 00:33:15.730
I'm not the lead pastor in a community,

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:17.870
but I go to all of the
different communities,

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:20.280
and I speak at a number of churches.

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:22.350
And so what is it like?

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:27.270
Well, yeah, people are, people don't,

00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:30.070
church is supposed to be
a place where they hear

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:31.650
about the love of God for them.

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:33.686
And they hear about the sacrifice,

00:33:33.686 --> 00:33:38.686
and they remember the
Lord's passion, et cetera.

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:42.110
And so it's kind of become,

00:33:42.110 --> 00:33:44.900
in some circles, in some churches,

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:48.933
the gospel has totally
become about current events.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:52.863
And I think that that's
definitely problematic.

00:33:55.049 --> 00:33:57.743
It definitely become problematic where,

00:34:00.250 --> 00:34:02.330
in Christianity we teach, essentially,

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:03.630
that everybody's a priest.

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:08.530
In fact, the Protestant
reformation happened

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:11.690
because one of Martin Luther's 95 problems

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:14.290
with the Catholic church was
that you had to have a priest

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:15.430
to connect with God.

00:34:15.430 --> 00:34:17.990
And the New Testament is
replete with scriptures,

00:34:17.990 --> 00:34:20.180
about the priesthood of the believer.

00:34:20.180 --> 00:34:24.130
And so what this necessarily
means for Protestants like me,

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:27.310
is that every believer is
supposedly coming to church,

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:28.980
to be a priest, that's
what you literally do.

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:30.610
So you come to what a priest do,

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:33.000
They minister to the Lord,
and they minister to others.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:34.860
But like when you come to church,

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:37.303
and it's a lesson on critical theory,

00:34:38.140 --> 00:34:40.663
you know, and there's
like, there's no escape.

00:34:41.575 --> 00:34:44.800
I think that people aren't
able to do their job,

00:34:44.800 --> 00:34:45.990
as a Christian.

00:34:45.990 --> 00:34:49.017
I think that it's really
tough to glorify God,

00:34:49.017 --> 00:34:51.150
and to worship God when it's like,

00:34:51.150 --> 00:34:53.520
you're in a perpetual classroom,

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:55.380
And there's just doubt
and there's confusion.

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:58.030
And I think that one of my
biggest frustrations for me

00:34:58.030 --> 00:34:59.920
is that I don't think that
a lot of church leaders

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:03.360
have their pulse on this
epistemological issue.

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:04.301
So it's washing,

00:35:04.301 --> 00:35:09.301
like pastors are good
people and they love people.

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:12.450
And so from that good place
of going, "I love people,"

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:14.300
they're probably going, "we
need to talk about this,

00:35:14.300 --> 00:35:15.670
because this is a real,

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:18.660
this is, this is where
the rubber hits the road."

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:20.177
But problematically, if you're not,

00:35:20.177 --> 00:35:23.350
you don't have your pulse on culture,

00:35:23.350 --> 00:35:27.260
and understanding the
undercurrents epistemologically,

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:29.107
you know, you're creating a
lot of confusion for people.

00:35:29.107 --> 00:35:31.007
So that's absolutely what's happening.

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:34.620
- Do you think certain
religious denominations

00:35:34.620 --> 00:35:36.610
and certain sex within religions,

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:40.750
are just better at keeping
some of this stuff at bay?

00:35:40.750 --> 00:35:42.700
So it seems to me it's not perfect,

00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:45.340
but like something like
Orthodox Jews, let's say,

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:47.610
have really kept this at bay in a way

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:50.810
that conservative or reformed
Jews haven't been able to.

00:35:50.810 --> 00:35:53.670
Or evangelicals, I think,
have done a pretty good job

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:55.530
at keeping it at bay.

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:57.560
Do you think that there
are like certain strengths

00:35:57.560 --> 00:36:00.120
and weaknesses from a
Protestant perspective,

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:02.340
or another denomination perspective,

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:05.820
that makes certain
worldviews a little more able

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:07.890
to stand up to these things?

00:36:07.890 --> 00:36:10.560
- Absolutely, so it's a focus,

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:13.400
it's a focus on their purpose.

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:16.700
Like, why do Orthodox Jews exist, right?

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:17.823
Like they know why.

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:21.180
And I mean, look,

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:23.440
break down sometimes to
the way that they dress,

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:25.900
like they're in this world,
but they're not of this world,

00:36:25.900 --> 00:36:27.580
sort of as Jesus said.

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:31.370
And so I think that they
do a really great job,

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:34.530
you know, of like focusing
on their community,

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:36.993
and focusing on the
reasons why they exist.

00:36:37.905 --> 00:36:41.380
So there is a definitely a balance to be,

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:43.670
see what I look at Jesus, and once again,

00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:45.610
this is a epistemological issue.

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:46.870
But when I look at Jesus,

00:36:46.870 --> 00:36:49.310
I don't look at somebody
who is necessarily engaged

00:36:49.310 --> 00:36:50.820
in the politics of his day.

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:52.017
I think that Jesus said,

00:36:52.017 --> 00:36:53.800
"My kingdom's not of this world."

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:56.010
And so he had a little bit of,

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:58.785
I'm in the world, but I'm not of it.

00:36:58.785 --> 00:37:03.670
Does that mean that we don't
engage in issues of justice?

00:37:03.670 --> 00:37:05.870
No, it doesn't mean that we don't.

00:37:05.870 --> 00:37:08.860
I think that marching
with Martin Luther King

00:37:08.860 --> 00:37:11.035
was the right thing to do,

00:37:11.035 --> 00:37:12.970
during the civil rights.

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:17.600
And I think that defeating Hitler

00:37:17.600 --> 00:37:19.463
was probably a good thing to do.

00:37:20.723 --> 00:37:25.723
I think that the church
doesn't run from things,

00:37:26.240 --> 00:37:28.200
but it's finding,

00:37:28.200 --> 00:37:30.440
it's sifting through the things
that we should engage in.

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:32.450
How we should engage, and
the threshold of that,

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:34.250
and it's a conversation
that we're having right now.

00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:37.210
And yeah, evangelicals probably have done

00:37:37.210 --> 00:37:39.660
a really great job because evangelicals

00:37:39.660 --> 00:37:41.750
are a bit more heady.

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:43.190
Pentecostals are probably a bit more,

00:37:43.190 --> 00:37:44.943
we lean a bit more experiential.

00:37:50.810 --> 00:37:53.640
I wish that Pentecostals were a bit more

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:58.310
like Evangelicals, in that they sifted,

00:37:58.310 --> 00:37:59.808
they had their, like Albert Mohler,

00:37:59.808 --> 00:38:03.700
he's the president of the
Southern Baptist Convention.

00:38:03.700 --> 00:38:07.750
And he sent out a letter
on critical theory,

00:38:07.750 --> 00:38:09.140
to all of the pastors,
and look, "Hey guys,

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:09.973
this is what's happening."

00:38:09.973 --> 00:38:11.520
Like the Pentecostals

